  description: "This comprehensive four-hour continuing education course prepares mental health professionals to deliver competent, compassionate, and evidence-based care to older adults experiencing neurocognitive disorders and their family caregivers. As the global population ages and the prevalence of dementia continues to rise, counselors increasingly encounter clients navigating the complex emotional, relational, and existential challenges associated with progressive cognitive decline. This course bridges the gap between clinical knowledge and practical application, equipping practitioners with the assessment skills, therapeutic interventions, ethical frameworks, and cultural considerations necessary to serve this vulnerable and growing population effectively. Participants will explore the full spectrum of neurocognitive disorders, including Alzheimer\u2019s disease, vascular dementia, Lewy body dementia, and frontotemporal dementia, while developing nuanced clinical skills for distinguishing normal aging from pathological decline. The course examines person-centered approaches to assessment and treatment planning, evidence-based interventions such as Cognitive Stimulation Therapy, reminiscence therapy, validation therapy, and music-based interventions. Significant attention is devoted to the caregiver experience, including the unique grief processes of ambiguous loss, compassion fatigue, and evidence-based caregiver support interventions. Ethical dimensions including capacity assessment, advance directives, autonomy versus beneficence tensions, end-of-life counseling considerations, and the identification and response to elder abuse are addressed through clinical vignettes featuring the Chen family, whose evolving story illustrates the progression of caregiving challenges and therapeutic responses across all eight modules.",
